    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n13006" root="دلهم" body-key="d">
    
      <b><span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">دَلْهَمٌ</span></b>  <i>Dark.</i> (K.) You say <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">لَيْلٌ دَلْهَمٌ</span> <i>Dark
night:</i> (TA:) and ↓ <b><span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">لَيْلَةٌ مُدْلَهِمَّةٌ</span></b>  <i>A dark night.</i>
(S, TA.) ― 
       <p class="alpheios-lex-sense-separator"> </p><span class="alpheios-lex-sense-spacer2"> </span>-b2-  And <i>Deprived of his reason by love:</i>
(K:) a signification which shows the <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">م</span> to be
augmentative; for it is from <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">الدَّلَهُ</span>: or, accord. to
IKtt and others, the <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">ل</span> in ↓ <b><span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">ادلهمّ</span></b>  is augmentative;
for, they say, it is from <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">الدُّهْمَةُ</span>: either opinion is
allowable. (TA.) ― 
       <p class="alpheios-lex-sense-separator"> </p><span class="alpheios-lex-sense-spacer2"> </span>-b3-  Also The <i>wolf.</i> (K.) ― 
       <p class="alpheios-lex-sense-separator"> </p><span class="alpheios-lex-sense-spacer2"> </span>-b4- 
And The <i>male of</i> [<i>the bird called</i>] <i>the</i> <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">قَطَا</span> [like
<span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">دَيْلَمٌ</span>]. (K.)

